## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to augment a balanced BST to support fast range queries on a multiset with duplicates—a fundamental interview pattern demonstrating understanding of data structure augmentation.

## Key Concepts
- **Treap structure**: Randomized BST with O(log n) expected height
- **Augmented nodes**: cnt tracks duplicates, size maintains subtree element counts
- **Range queries**: countLE(x) finds rank ≤ x; rangeCount derived as countLE(r) - countLE(l-1)
- **Efficient updates**: Size rebalancing occurs only on paths to modified nodes

## Code Example
```python
import random

class TreapNode:
    def __init__(self, key):
        self.key = key
        self.cnt = 1
        self.size = 1
        self.prio = random.random()
        self.left = None
        self.right = None

def update_size(node):
    if node:
        node.size = node.cnt
        if node.left:
            node.size += node.left.size
        if node.right:
            node.size += node.right.size

def rotate_right(p):
    q = p.left
    p.left = q.right
    q.right = p
    update_size(p)
    update_size(q)
    return q

def rotate_left(p):
    q = p.right
    p.right = q.left
    q.left = p
    update_size(p)
    update_size(q)
    return q

def insert(node, key):
    if not node:
        return TreapNode(key)

    if key == node.key:
        node.cnt += 1
    elif key < node.key:
        node.left = insert(node.left, key)
        if node.left.prio < node.prio:
            node = rotate_right(node)
    else:
        node.right = insert(node.right, key)
        if node.right.prio < node.prio:
            node = rotate_left(node)

    update_size(node)
    return node

def count_le(node, x):
    if not node:
        return 0

    if node.key <= x:
        left_size = node.left.size if node.left else 0
        return left_size + node.cnt + count_le(node.right, x)
    else:
        return count_le(node.left, x)

def range_count(node, l, r):
    return count_le(node, r) - count_le(node, l - 1)
```

## Why This Is Asked
Dynamic maximum XOR subset queries with deletions present a non-trivial challenge. An offline approach using interval decomposition elegantly handles deletions by converting them into active intervals.

## Key Concepts
- Time-segment tree over operation timeline
- Rollbackable linear basis for XOR operations
- Handling duplicates through separate active intervals

## Code Example

```javascript
class LinearBasis {
  constructor() { 
    this.b = new Array(32).fill(0); 
    this.history = [];
  }

  insert(x) {
    const original = [...this.b];
    this.history.push(original);

    for (let i = 31; i >= 0; i--) {
      if (!(x >> i) & 1) continue;
      if (!this.b[i]) {
        this.b[i] = x;
        break;
      }
      x ^= this.b[i];
    }
  }

  rollback() {
    this.b = this.history.pop();
  }

  maxXor() {
    let result = 0;
    for (let i = 31; i >= 0; i--) {
      result = Math.max(result, result ^ this.b[i]);
    }
    return result;
  }
}

class SegmentTree {
  constructor(n) {
    this.size = 1;
    while (this.size < n) this.size *= 2;
    this.tree = Array(2 * this.size).fill().map(() => []);
  }

  addInterval(l, r, value) {
    for (l += this.size, r += this.size; l < r; l >>= 1, r >>= 1) {
      if (l & 1) this.tree[l++].push(value);
      if (r & 1) this.tree[--r].push(value);
    }
  }

  process(queries) {
    const results = [];
    const basis = new LinearBasis();

    const dfs = (node, nodeL, nodeR) => {
      const saveHistorySize = basis.history.length;

      for (const value of this.tree[node]) {
        basis.insert(value);
      }

      if (nodeR - nodeL === 1) {
        results[nodeL] = basis.maxXor();
      } else {
        const mid = (nodeL + nodeR) / 2;
        dfs(node * 2, nodeL, mid);
        dfs(node * 2 + 1, mid, nodeR);
      }

      while (basis.history.length > saveHistorySize) {
        basis.rollback();
      }
    };

    dfs(1, 0, this.size);
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Update Rules
- Insert(x): Map to interval [time, ∞) until erase
- Erase(x): Close interval at current time
- Query: Process at leaf, gather basis maxXor result
- Rollback: Restore basis to previous state after subtree processing

## Complexity
- Build: O(n log n) for interval decomposition
- Process: O(32 log n) per query (32-bit basis × segment tree depth)
- Memory: O(n log n) for storing intervals in segment tree

This solution efficiently handles the dynamic nature with deletions by leveraging interval decomposition and rollback mechanisms, providing optimal query performance while maintaining clean separation of concerns.

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to implement streaming statistics with efficient data structures and real-time median calculation.

## Key Concepts

- Dual heap structure (max-heap + min-heap)
- Balance invariant maintenance
- Median computation in O(log n) per insert
- Streaming data processing

## Code Example

```javascript
class MedianStream {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();  // Lower half
    this.high = new MinHeap(); // Upper half
  }

  insert(x) {
    // Insert into appropriate heap and rebalance
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || x <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(x);
    } else {
      this.high.push(x);
    }

    // Rebalance to maintain size difference ≤ 1
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size() + 1) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  getMedian() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) {
      return this.low.peek();
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) {
      return this.high.peek();
    } else {
      return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
    }
  }
}
```

## Implementation Notes

- Duplicates are handled naturally by heap properties
- Memory usage is O(n) for storing all elements
- Suitable for real-time streaming applications
- Can be extended to support removal operations with additional bookkeeping

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of order statistics in dynamic multisets with duplicates and efficient prefix queries—skills fundamental to database indexing and ranking problems.

## Key Concepts
- **Balanced BST with per-node counts and subtree sizes** enables O(log n) operations
- **Handling duplicates via dupCount** avoids unnecessary node creation
- **getRank as a prefix-count query** is analogous to order statistics

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(key) {
    this.key = key;
    this.dup = 1;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }

  get size() {
    return this.dup + (this.left?.size ?? 0) + (this.right?.size ?? 0);
  }
}

class Treap {
  constructor() {
    this.root = null;
  }

  insert(key) {
    this.root = this._insert(this.root, key);
  }

  _insert(node, key) {
    if (!node) return new Node(key);
    if (key === node.key) {
      node.dup++;
    } else if (key < node.key) {
      node.left = this._insert(node.left, key);
    } else {
      node.right = this._insert(node.right, key);
    }
    return node;
  }

  erase(key) {
    this.root = this._erase(this.root, key);
  }

  _erase(node, key) {
    if (!node) return null;
    if (key === node.key) {
      node.dup--;
      if (node.dup === 0) return this._merge(node.left, node.right);
    } else if (key < node.key) {
      node.left = this._erase(node.left, key);
    } else {
      node.right = this._erase(node.right, key);
    }
    return node;
  }

  _merge(left, right) {
    if (!left || !right) return left ?? right;
    // Simplified: in a real Treap, merge by priority
    return this._bstMerge(left, right);
  }

  _bstMerge(left, right) {
    if (!left) return right;
    if (!right) return left;
    const node = left;
    node.right = this._bstMerge(node.right, right);
    return node;
  }

  getRank(v) {
    return this._getRank(this.root, v);
  }

  _getRank(node, v) {
    if (!node) return 0;
    if (v < node.key) {
      return this._getRank(node.left, v);
    }
    const leftSize = node.left?.size ?? 0;
    return leftSize + node.dup + this._getRank(node.right, v);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a mutable deque with constant-time min/max queries, a common interview pattern for surface-level data-structure mastery.

## Key Concepts
- Deque operations and invariants
- Two-stack technique for amortized efficiency
- Maintaining per-element min/max for O(1) queries
- Edge-case handling (empty, duplicates)

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinMaxDeque {
  constructor() {
    this.front = [];
    this.back = [];
  }
  pushFront(x) {
    const last = this.front[this.front.length - 1];
    const mn = last ? Math.min(x, last.min) : x;
    const mx = last ? Math.max(x, last.max) : x;
    this.front.push({ v: x, min: mn, max: mx });
  }
  pushBack(x) {
    const last = this.back[this.back.length - 1];
    const mn = last ? Math.min(x, last.min) : x;
    const mx = last ? Math.max(x, last.max) : x;
    this.back.push({ v: x, min: mn, max: mx });
  }
  popFront() {
    if (this.front.length === 0) {
      while (this.back.length) this.pushFront(this.back.pop().v);
    }
    if (this.front.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.front.pop().v;
  }
  popBack() {
    if (this.back.length === 0) {
      while (this.front.length) this.pushBack(this.front.pop().v);
    }
    if (this.back.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.back.pop().v;
  }
  getMin() {
    if (this.front.length === 0 && this.back.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    let mn = null;
    if (this.front.length) mn = this.front[this.front.length - 1].min;
    if (this.back.length) {
      const bmin = this.back[this.back.length - 1].min;
      mn = mn === null ? bmin : Math.min(mn, bmin);
    }
    return mn;
  }
  getMax() {
    if (this.front.length === 0 && this.back.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    let mx = null;
    if (this.front.length) mx = this.front[this.front.length - 1].max;
    if (this.back.length) {
      const bmax = this.back[this.back.length - 1].max;
      mx = mx === null ? bmax : Math.max(mx, bmax);
    }
    return mx;
  }
}
```

